*******************************************************************************
* The following .include lines are necessary for all your CSCI 50.02 JSim
* exercises. These work like your Java import statement.
*
* TL;DR: Import area, do not touch.
*******************************************************************************

.include "8clocks.jsim"
.include "nominal.jsim"
.include "stdcell.jsim"


*******************************************************************************
* Reference Guide:
*
* What follows is a (one-line) statement that describes our first "Hello
* World!" circuit. This statement is in the form:
*
*   Xid a z inverter
*
* Xid is just the name we give to this circuit block (in this case, Xinv).
* a is an input node ("clk1" is a 10ns-period clock defined in 8clocks.jsim).
* z is an output node (the name "out" is just something we made up).
* inverter is the type of subcircuit that we want (defined in stdcell.jsim).
*******************************************************************************

*******************************************************************************
* "Define a .subckt named ''fx'' for the function OUT = AB + CDE + FG"
*
* Description:
* We begin with double-negating the whole equation at the start. Since !!X = X, nothing is affected at this stage.
* !!(AB + CDE + FG)
* By doing this, we are setting up De Morgan's Laws which allows us to inverted the whole circuit and utilize NAND circuits.
* However, we will only do this with the first negation (!) we added.
* !(!(AB) * !(CDE) * !(FG))
* From here, we have the circuit as its simplest. As, for example, X1 is !(AB) which is equivalent to NAND(A,B).
* The same applies to X2 and X3. This allows us to use only NANDs for X1, X2, and X3.
* For X4 below, it can be read as !( X1 * X2 * X3) which is another 3 input NAND.
* Utilizing NANDs here provides us with the solution utilizing the fewest transistors.
* Analyzing the circuit without De Morgan's would lead us to think and utilize AND and ORs when designing this solution, resulting in more 'transistors' being used.
*******************************************************************************

.subckt fx a b c d e f g out
X1  a b     n_ab    nand2
X2  c d e   n_cde   nand3
X3  f g     n_fg    nand2

X4  n_ab n_cde n_fg out nand3
.ends

Xfx clk1 clk2 clk3 clk4 clk5 clk6 clk7 out fx

*******************************************************************************
* "Define a .subckt named 'fa' for a full adder circuit"
*
* Description:
* In designing a full adder, we know that the following must be present:
* Sum = A xor B xor C_in
* C_out = AB + AC_in + BC_in or AB + C_in(A xor B)
* For the purposes of our expalantion, we will use the 2nd one for C_out.
* Having worked with half-adders before, that Sum is equivalent to a half adder xor C_in.
* The half adder would be the A xor B part of the Sum.
*
* During this process, we designed a version of the solution for NAND and XOR.
* While designing the 2 versions, we noted that we needed 4 NANDs to replicate an XOR.
* For A xor B, we'd need to make NAND((nand(a, nand(A,B)), (nand(n, nand(A,B))).
* Looking back at the previous lab, we know that a xor2 would use 12 mosfets.
* For the case of nand, we know a nand2 uses 4 mosfets.
* For just A XOR B, we'd be looking at 16 (4 instances of nand2) over the 12 of XOR.
* The version of the circuit we designed using NANDs had 9 total NANDs used (totaling to 36 mosfets).
* For the XOR version, we utilized 2 XORs and 3 NANDs (totaling to 36).
* Mosfet usage wise, they are identical by the end.
*
* In the end, we decided to go with XOR because it uses lets gates
* Although utilizing the same MOSFETs by the end, XOR utilizies less gates.
* Utilizing only 5 vesus the NAND versions' 9, we'd be cutting out 4 more.
* It makes it an easier design to verify and construct, thus debug, over the multiple moving parts the NAND version does.
*
* For your convenience, the NAND version is included but commented out below:
*
* .subckt fa2 a b carry_in sum carry
* X1 a b n1 nand2
* X2 a n1 n2 nand2
* X3 b n1 n3 nand2
* X4 n2 n3 n4 nand2

* X5 n4 carry_in n5 nand2
* X6 n4 n5 n6 nand2
* X7 carry_in n5 n7 nand2
* X8 n6 n7 sum nand2

* X9 n1 n5 carry nand2
* .ends
*
*******************************************************************************

.subckt fa a b carry_in sum carry
X1 a b xor_ xor2
X2 xor_ carry_in sum xor2

X3 a b n_ab nand2
X4 xor_ carry_in n_xor1_carry_in nand2
X5 n_ab n_xor1_carry_in carry nand2
.ends

Xfa clk1 clk2 clk3 sum carry fa

*******************************************************************************
* The circuit above does NOT display anything in the simulator. We tell JSim
* to display something via .tran and .plot commands. The .tran statement
* specifies the period of time (the "transient") that we want to plot.
* Afterwards, we just .plot the nodes that we are interested in.
*
* In this example, we plot the input and the output of the Xinv circuit in two
* separate "plot channels". (You can plot both signals in one channel by saying
* ".plot clk1 out" instead, but it will be hard to see any overlaps in signal.)
*******************************************************************************

.tran 1000ns
.plot clk1
.plot clk2
.plot clk3
.plot clk4
.plot clk5
.plot clk6
.plot clk7
.plot _
.plot out
.plot _
.plot sum
.plot carry

*******************************************************************************
* To actually see these plots, click the "Gate-level Simulation" button on the
* toolbar (fourth button from the right, the one that looks like an AND gate).
* This opens a separate Plot Window.
*
* While these .jsim files need to be opened in JSim to perform the simulations,
* we HIGHLY recommend that you edit these files in a full-fledged text editor
* instead of from within JSim's own editor, because the JSim editor does NOT
* have undo/redo functions. I personally recommend Visual Studio Code:
*
*   https://code.visualstudio.com/
*
* Then, when you want to see your changes on the Plot Window, simply click the
* "Reload Files" button (the button with the green looping arrows on a sheet of
* paper), then re-simulate by clicking the "Gate-level Simulation" button (the
* Plot Window has its own). It is NOT enough to click just one of the two
* buttons -- you have to click Reload, then Simulation, in that order!
*
* Happy hacking! - eric
*******************************************************************************
